# Review of the proposed ATAG Working Group charter

**Reviewed:** November 25, 2026  
**Charter:** [Proposed Authoring Tool Accessibility Guidelines Working Group Charter](https://www.w3.org/2026/11/proposed-atag-wg-charter.html)

## Verdict

**Recommendation: recharter with changes, including a one-year checkpoint.** Updating ATAG is a worthwhile W3C effort: ATAG 2.0 still points to WCAG 2.0, and accessible authoring tools can affect both disabled authors and the accessibility of the content they produce. Accessibility Standards Canada is also developing an ATAG 3.0 draft. But the proposed two-year charter guarantees only a First Public Working Draft; its other new-version milestones are described as non-deadlines, and the previous WG took years longer than planned to complete ATAG 2.0. Before approval, make the timetable and checkpoint meaningful, clarify what the WG will deliver for testing and implementation, and resolve how the Document License works with ASC’s separate standard. [Proposed charter](https://www.w3.org/2026/11/proposed-atag-wg-charter.html), [ATAG publication history](https://www.w3.org/standards/history/ATAG20/), [ASC ATAG 3.0 draft listing](https://accessible.canada.ca/standards-and-technical-guides/standards-and-technical-guides-database/authoring-tool-accessibility-guidelines-atag-30-can-asc-63).

This review is based on the charter’s AC review copy available **November 25, 2026**. The strategy issue shows that AC review began November 17; the charter itself still has placeholder start and end dates. [Strategy issue #552](https://github.com/w3c/strategy/issues/552), [proposed charter](https://www.w3.org/2026/11/proposed-atag-wg-charter.html).

## 1. Baseline and milestone history

The prior ATAG WG closed in 2015, so this is a return to work after a long gap, rather than a routine continuation of an active group. The closest baselines are its 2004 and 2010 charters. Both promised ATAG 2.0 as a Recommendation; the 2010 charter set detailed publication dates. [WG charter history](https://www.w3.org/groups/wg/atag/charters/), [2004 charter](https://www.w3.org/WAI/AU/charter5-20040116.html), [2010 charter](https://www.w3.org/WAI/AU/2010/auwg_charter.html).

| Deliverable or stage | Earlier promise | Actual result | Finding |
|---|---|---|---|
| ATAG 2.0 Recommendation | The 2004 charter scheduled a 24-month group period, January 2005–December 2006; it was extended in 2008. The 2010 charter then targeted a Recommendation in May 2011. | ATAG 2.0 became a Recommendation on September 24, 2015. | Nearly nine years after the 2004 charter’s original period, and more than four years after the 2010 target. |
| Last Call | June 2010 in the 2010 charter | September 10, 2013 | About three years late. |
| Candidate Recommendation | September 2010 in the 2010 charter | November 7, 2013 | About three years late. |
| Proposed Recommendation | March 2011 in the 2010 charter | July 21, 2015 | More than four years late. |
| ATAG 3.0 | The proposed charter targets requirements in Q3 2027 and FPWD in Q2 2028; expected completion is Working Draft. | Not started as a WG deliverable. | No dated milestone for a later draft, test report, or stable specification. |

The historical publication dates come from W3C’s [ATAG 2.0 history](https://www.w3.org/standards/history/ATAG20/). The old group’s charter history records repeated extensions through 2015. [Charter history](https://www.w3.org/groups/wg/atag/charters/).

There is some evidence about why the old work slipped. The ATAG 2.0 implementation report describes scarce development resources and delays adapting its test harness, including security work and reporting bugs. The record does not establish that these were the only or main causes of the full schedule slip. [Implementation report](https://www.w3.org/WAI/AU/CR20/ImplementationReport).

## 2. Need, adoption, and timeliness by deliverable

ATAG is a guideline, so browser support counters are not an appropriate adoption measure. The relevant evidence is conformance, public-sector or procurement use, implementer participation, and whether evaluation resources are used.

| Deliverable | Real problem | Deployment and impact | Timeliness |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Next ATAG version** | **Strong.** ATAG 2.0 references WCAG 2.0, which was current when ATAG 2.0 was published in 2015. The problem is especially current for AI-assisted authoring: ASC identifies risks both for disabled authors using AI tools and for people consuming inaccessible AI-generated content. [WAI ATAG overview](https://www.w3.org/WAI/standards-guidelines/atag/), [ASC draft](https://accessible.canada.ca/standards-and-technical-guides/standards-and-technical-guides-database/authoring-tool-accessibility-guidelines-atag-30-can-asc-63). | **Not yet demonstrated for a new version.** No ATAG 3.0 result exists. ASC lists a draft standard, but its public page gives no posting or publication date and says technical committee members are “to be established.” [ASC draft status](https://accessible.canada.ca/standards-and-technical-guides/standards-and-technical-guides-database/authoring-tool-accessibility-guidelines-atag-30-can-asc-63). | **Weakly specified.** Q2 2028 is the only dated milestone for ATAG 3.0, and the charter expressly says dates are not deadlines. The WG’s past schedule history makes a checkpoint useful. [Proposed charter](https://www.w3.org/2026/11/proposed-atag-wg-charter.html), [ATAG 2.0 history](https://www.w3.org/standards/history/ATAG20/). |
| **Maintain ATAG 2.0** | **Clear but bounded.** Updating outdated references and definitions is sensible; WAI notes that ATAG 2.0 refers to WCAG 2.0 and recommends using the latest WCAG. [WAI overview](https://www.w3.org/WAI/standards-guidelines/atag/). | **Some evidence, but limited.** ATAG 2.0 has a historical implementation report and evaluation tools. EN 301 549 cites ATAG 2.0 as information of interest to authoring-tool creators, but its authoring-tool clause is informative. This shows a point of reference, not broad conformance or a binding ATAG requirement. [Implementation report](https://www.w3.org/WAI/AU/CR20/ImplementationReport), [ETSI EN 301 549](https://www.etsi.org/deliver/etsi_en/301500_301599/301549/03.02.01_60/en_301549v030201p.pdf). | **Undated as a maintenance outcome.** The charter says it will focus on outdated references, definitions, and informative content; Q2 2027 is an editorial-revision target, but not a deadline. [Proposed charter](https://www.w3.org/2026/11/proposed-atag-wg-charter.html). |
| **Maintain Implementing ATAG 2.0** | **Plausible.** The supporting Note explains the requirements and provides examples, but also reflects the age of ATAG 2.0. [Implementing ATAG 2.0](https://www.w3.org/TR/IMPLEMENTING-ATAG20/). | **A live support resource, with no current use data found.** WAI provides an ATAG Report Tool and an authoring-tools list. Those are useful operational resources, but their availability does not show how widely they are used or whether current products claim ATAG conformance. [ATAG overview and resources](https://www.w3.org/WAI/standards-guidelines/atag/), [tool submission page](https://www.w3.org/WAI/tools-list/authoring/submit-a-tool/). | **No distinct deadline.** It is a maintenance commitment, without a specific dated outcome in the charter. |
| **Possible use cases, tests, implementation report, primer, or best practices** | **Potentially strong**, particularly if these make guidance applicable to modern and AI-assisted tools. | **Uncommitted.** The charter says the WG *may* create these materials. W3C’s earlier report tested 25 tools, but that evidence is from the ATAG 2.0 Candidate Recommendation period, not current adoption. [Implementation report](https://www.w3.org/WAI/AU/CR20/ImplementationReport). | **Unmeasurable as drafted.** These outputs are optional and have no milestones. |

ATAG 2.0’s historical implementation report is meaningful evidence that the guidelines could be tested: it reports 25 tools tested, including 16 fully tested, across several tool categories. It is not evidence of present-day conformance. The report also says Drupal and WordPress were too complex to test with the available resources; that does not establish that they failed ATAG. [Implementation report](https://www.w3.org/WAI/AU/CR20/ImplementationReport).

For regulatory and procurement uptake, the evidence is mixed. EN 301 549 points to ATAG as informative material, while the U.S. Section 508 program has its own authoring-tool requirements. ASC’s ATAG 3.0 work could create a stronger adoption route, but the public record describes it as a draft with no publication date. [ETSI EN 301 549](https://www.etsi.org/deliver/etsi_en/301500_301599/301549/03.02.01_60/en_301549v030201p.pdf), [Section 508 authoring-tool requirements](https://www.section508.gov/buy/authoring-tools/), [ASC draft status](https://accessible.canada.ca/standards-and-technical-guides/standards-and-technical-guides-database/authoring-tool-accessibility-guidelines-atag-30-can-asc-63).

## 3. Work outside the charter and overlap

The proposed WG has not yet begun work, so I found no evidence of current WG activity outside its charter. Its plans do intersect with other W3C work:

- The Accessibility Guidelines WG maintains errata for ATAG 2.0, while stating that it is not doing substantive ATAG work. This makes the proposed WG’s separate ATAG 2.0 maintenance role reasonable, but the boundary should be explicit. [AG WG charter](https://www.w3.org/2023/11/ag-charter).
- WCAG 3.0 addresses related tools such as content-management systems, authoring tools, and testing tools, while the AG WG says WCAG 3 will not include normative guidance for authoring tools. The proposed charter should state how the groups will keep the boundary and references aligned. [WCAG 3.0 Working Draft](https://www.w3.org/TR/wcag3/), [AG WG charter](https://www.w3.org/2023/11/ag-charter).
- The WAI report tool was discussed as a possible resource for future ATAG work, but the charter does not name it as a specific deliverable. If the WG is expected to maintain or replace it, state that directly. [ATAG CG February 2026 minutes](https://www.w3.org/2026/02/13-atag-minutes.html).

There is also a scope boundary to sharpen: the charter includes guidance for AI- or automation-driven tools and “the content they produce,” while listing standards for content accessibility as out of scope. Clarify that the WG can specify how authoring tools support accessible output without taking on WCAG’s separate role defining content accessibility.

