## 5. Charter quality and licensing

**Schedule and outputs.** The ATAG 3.0 work is expected to reach only Working Draft during this charter. The timeline has dates, but says they are not deadlines; later stages have no target dates. The test suite and implementation report are optional even though the charter says a normative specification must have an open test suite to advance beyond Candidate Recommendation. Add a dated decision point and say what evidence will determine whether to continue toward Candidate Recommendation.

**Success criteria.** “Two independent interoperable implementations” is not a natural measure for guidelines. The charter should define how it will test and report conformance criteria for a guideline, including who evaluates tools, how results will be shared, and what constitutes adequate implementation evidence. The ATAG 2.0 process used an implementation report and testing criteria; those can inform a suitable method. [Proposed charter](https://www.w3.org/2026/11/proposed-atag-wg-charter.html), [ATAG 2.0 implementation report](https://www.w3.org/WAI/AU/CR20/ImplementationReport).

**Participation and staffing.** The charter names two invited-expert chairs and W3C team contacts totaling 0.09 FTE. It does not set an expected number of active participants or specify representation from authoring-tool developers, disability communities, or procurement bodies. The ASC draft listing says its technical committee members are “to be established,” while October meeting minutes describe ASC expertise and a recently signed MOU. Resolve the public status and expected participation in the joint work. [Proposed charter](https://www.w3.org/2026/11/proposed-atag-wg-charter.html), [ASC draft](https://accessible.canada.ca/standards-and-technical-guides/standards-and-technical-guides-database/authoring-tool-accessibility-guidelines-atag-30-can-asc-63), [October 29 minutes](https://www.w3.org/2026/10/29-atag-minutes.html).

There is a small internal schedule inconsistency: the charter header says teleconferences are weekly, while its Communication section says meetings will be held as needed. Reconcile those statements. The start and end dates and the new-charter history row also still contain template placeholders; replace them in the final approved charter. [Proposed charter](https://www.w3.org/2026/11/proposed-atag-wg-charter.html).

**Patent policy.** The charter uses the W3C Patent Policy version of May 15, 2025. Under that policy, the FPWD opens a 150-day opportunity for participants to exclude essential claims; the specification licensing commitment becomes effective at the later of the first Patent Review Draft or Recommendation and the end of the applicable exclusion period. Since this charter expects only a Working Draft, it should be clear that the first draft provides early disclosure and review, while the full RF commitment is tied to later stages. [Proposed charter](https://www.w3.org/2026/11/proposed-atag-wg-charter.html), [W3C Patent Policy](https://www.w3.org/policies/patent-policy/).

**Document license.** The proposal selects the W3C Document License for *all* deliverables. The W3C charter template uses the Software and Document License as its default. The Document License permits some implementation-related derivatives but expressly prohibits publishing derivative works as technical specifications. This matters because the charter says W3C and ASC will jointly develop guidelines under an MOU, and ASC lists a separate ATAG 3.0 standard. [Charter and license clause](https://www.w3.org/2026/11/proposed-atag-wg-charter.html), [charter template](https://w3c.github.io/charter-drafts/charter-template.html), [Document License](https://www.w3.org/copyright/document-license-2023/), [ASC listing](https://accessible.canada.ca/standards-and-technical-guides/standards-and-technical-guides-database/authoring-tool-accessibility-guidelines-atag-30-can-asc-63).

The 2004 and 2010 charters have no separate licensing section, so they do not provide a charter-level license history to compare. The concern here is the proposed arrangement with ASC: the linked MOU was not publicly accessible during this review, so I could not verify whether it grants the permissions needed to issue a separate ASC technical standard. October meeting minutes describe the MOU as signed and going through AC review. Request the signed terms or explicit licensing language before approval; use the Software and Document License, or document an adequate permission for ASC’s standard and reuse in informative materials. [2004 charter](https://www.w3.org/WAI/AU/charter5-20040116.html), [2010 charter](https://www.w3.org/WAI/AU/2010/auwg_charter.html), [October 29 minutes](https://www.w3.org/2026/10/29-atag-minutes.html).

## 6. Costs, benefits, and alternatives

The stated W3C staff commitment is small, but the WG will still draw on chairs, horizontal reviewers, the Advisory Committee, and external participants. Its value is a neutral standards venue, broad review, and a path to a W3C Recommendation with patent-policy commitments. Those benefits are most persuasive if the work can reach beyond an FPWD and produce evidence that implementers and purchasers can use.

- **Recharter with changes — recommended.** Keep the ATAG 3.0 work, add a one-year checkpoint and concrete outputs for requirements, implementation evaluation, and coordination with ASC. This preserves the chance of a joint standard while making continuation depend on visible progress.
- **Shorter or maintenance-focused charter.** Prioritize keeping ATAG 2.0 useful and deliver a requirements draft before committing to a wider rewrite. This lowers the risk of another long-running effort but delays broader modernization.
- **Continue incubation in the ATAG Community Group.** This reduces W3C Working Group and horizontal-review costs while requirements and participation mature. It gives up the WG’s Recommendation-track process and the associated patent-policy route for now.
- **Close the proposal.** This avoids new W3C commitments, but leaves the update to the CG and ASC, without a W3C-led Recommendation.

## 7. Changes to request in the AC comment

1. Add a one-year checkpoint with explicit questions: is there a usable requirements document, evidence of authoring-tool developer and disability-community participation, and a credible route from FPWD to later stages?
2. Replace “dates are not deadlines” with measurable target dates and add a milestone for the implementation report or evaluation method. Keep the Working Draft target if that is the intended two-year outcome, but state what must be true before the WG continues.
3. Clarify the boundary between authoring-tool guidance and standards for content accessibility, and specify how ATAG work relates to AGWG’s WCAG 3 work and ATAG 2.0 errata.
4. Reconcile the ASC draft’s committee status with the MOU account in the meeting minutes; provide the signed MOU or explain the copyright and publication rights for ASC’s separate standard.
5. Change the license to the W3C Software and Document License, or state the specific permission that allows ASC to publish a related technical specification. Clarify whether the license choice applies to all test materials and informative resources.
6. Set an expected participation level and target representation from authoring-tool implementers, people with disabilities, and procurement stakeholders. Reconcile the weekly-meeting statement with “as needed,” and fill the charter-date placeholders before final publication.
